; status: works — the `at` operator selecting one segment of a bent crease.
; --b is a flat diagonal precrease; the half-fold --v bends it at (½,½) into two
; segments on DIFFERENT lines — lower on y=x (flap .a .b), upper on x+y=1 (flap
; .c .d). Bare --b is now "not straight" and errors; `at #(.c .d)` names the upper
; segment, and perp-through-.a to it is a genuinely different axis than `at #(.a .b)`
; would give — that divergence is the whole point of `at`. (Crossing --b with --v
; instead would be vacuous: both segments meet --v at the same bend point.)
; No `moving`: folding the .c side is the default for `map .c onto …`.
paper square
mark --b = through .a .c
fold --v = map .c onto .b
mark --q = perp --b & #[.c .d] through .a

; assert faces = 4
; assert steps = 1
; assert --v is valley
; assert .a incident --q
; assert .c incident --q
